  "final_text": "Both now exist.\n\n**Data.** The Kibana Sample Data Logs view has these fields:\n- `timestamp`, the time field\n- `response`, a string, so I used `response.keyword`\n- `bytes`\n- `extension`\n- `clientip`\n- `geo.src` and `geo.dest`\n- `machine.os`\n- `url` and `request`\n- `host`\n- `agent`\n\n**Dashboard.** \"EFF-d2eea73e Web logs overview\" has four panels:\n- Total requests\n- Requests over time\n- Requests by response code\n- Average bytes by extension\n\nIt's at `http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-d2eea73e-web-logs-overview-1c8d0c6184dc`. I haven't opened it to check how the panels render.\n\n**Alert rule.** \"EFF-d2eea73e 5xx spike\" (id `13a84179-249d-48c7-a9b0-1b962b1f987f`) is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`:\n- It runs every minute and counts documents where `response.keyword` matches `5[0-9][0-9]`.\n- It fires when that count is above 10 within a 5-minute window.\n- I created it enabled, and its status was still \"pending\" when I created it. It has no action attached, so it will only show up as an alert in Kibana and won't notify anyone.\n- The sample data's timestamps may not be recent, so the rule may not fire until fresh data arrives.",
